  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 340 AM CDT Tue Jul 28 2026\n\nThe 08Z/3 am surface map shows a weak cold front near I-72.\nIsolated convection along it over our far northern CWA late Mon\nevening had dissipated a few hours ago and just some patchy\ncumulus clouds along and se of the boundary. Warm temps in the\nupper 70s to around 80F except 74 at Macomb and Galesburg. Moist\ndewpoints in the mid to upper 70s in central IL, though lower\n70s arriving nw of the IL river and around 80F dewpoints se of\nI-70. \n\nLatest CAMs push cold front across southeast IL by mid morning \nand reaching near the Ohio river by 15Z/10 am. Consensus of \nmodels keep our CWA dry with passage of cold front with winds \nturning northerly and slowly bringing in cooler and less humid \nair. MCS over southeast Nebraska to weaken as it moves se\nacross MO today keeping its convection west/sw of IL. Highs \ntoday in the mid to upper 80s (coolest from I-74 ne) while \ndewpoints settle into the upper 60s/lower 70s during this \nafternoon. Afternoon heat indices peak in the mid to upper 90s \nfrom Springfield to Effingham to Robinson sw with heat indices \ntopping 100F just south of CWA where heat advisory continues \ntoday for Marion, Wayne, Edwards and Wabash counties. Variably \ncloudy today with some passing smoke at higher levels from \nwildfires over the Pacific Northwest/northern Rockies. The near \nsurface smoke models show some diffuse smoke passing southward \nbehind cold front during this morning though current \nobservations over IL and nearby states not showing much \nrestriction to visibilities.\n\nMostly clear skies expected tonight with ample sunshine Wed as\nweak high pressure 1016-1018 mb settles down over WI and IL by \nWed morning. Comfy lows overnight and Tue night in the lower 60s\n(near 60F in east central IL) with highs Wed in the low to mid \n80s. Highs Thu in the mid 80s with weak surface high pressure\nstaying near IL with light winds. Slight chance of light rain\nshowers late Thu night over western CWA otherwise much of CWA\nremains dry thru Thu night. Seasonable lows Thu night in the mid\nto upper 60s.  \n\nMedium range models continue to show a decent northern stream\nshort wave ridging over top of mid/upper level ridge over the\ncentral and southern Rockies/Southwest States, that develops a\ncutoff low near MN/IA Fri morning. This to likely spread showers\nand isolated thunderstorms east/se over central IL by Friday\nafternoon with highest pops (50-70%) from I-55 west/nw. We have\n50-70% pops arriving over area Fri night and Saturday. WPC Day \n4 ERO has a marginal to slight risk of excessive rainfall Fri\nafternoon and Fri night, with the slight risk over western half\nof CWA. WPC Day5 ERO has marginal risk of excessive rainfall\nacross IL Sat/Sat evening with slight risk in far se IL. SPC and\nMachine Learning do not have any risk area for severe wx over IL\nlate this week as instability appears to be weak. Highs Fri in\nthe lower 80s (near 85F at Lawrenceville) and upper 70s to around\n80F on Sat. The GEM models is quicker weakening and exiting\nupper level low/trof se of IL by Sunday morning, though Ecmwf\nand GFS models linger weak cutoff 500 mb low over the mid\nMS/lower Ohio river valley on Sunday and Monday keeping chances\nof showers around especially se of the IL river. If cutoff low\nlingers nearby early next week, we would see cooler and wetter\nconditions than currently forecast by NBM projections.  \n\nThe Climate Prediction Center (CPC) 8-14 Day Outlook for Aug\n4-10th has a 45-55% chance of above normal temperatures over IL\nand precipitation trending near normal across the Prairie State.",
